<</Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/Alt (Common drivers of multi-region platform discussions Common drivers of multi-region platform discussions. Business growth and proximity: New markets or geographies; Acquisitions or divestitures; Products closer to customers; User latency. Data residency, sovereignty, and regulation: Data-residency laws; Operational resilience requirements; Customer contracts; Public-sector requirements. Service, AI model, SKU, and quota availability: AI models and GPU SKUs; Services in selected regions only; Quota constraints; Availability-zone requirements. Resiliency and concentration risk: RTO or RPO requirements; Concentration risk; Paired or nonpaired regions; Audit or regulatory findings; Lessons from incidents. Datacenter and connectivity events: Datacenter or colocation exits; Migration waves; Office or datacenter moves; Network-provider changes; New Azure region in the geography. Cost and operating model: Regional price differences; Cross-region transfer cost; Landing-zone redesign; Unnecessary infrastructure; Sustainability objectives. Drivers open the discussion. They do not determine the regional architecture or the workload-placement outcome. What decides the outcome: Qualify regions, Select the regional design, Prepare the region, Place workloads.)
/P 1258 0 R
/Pg 220 0 R
/K 4>>
